 s+=table(['N80 expected 95% CI half-width','Faithful prevalence assumption','κ','Accuracy','AC1'],rows)
 s+='Central simulation: true κ = 0.75, accuracy = 0.85, ICC = 0.25; 200 panels per scenario. Across κ 0.70–0.80 and accuracy 0.85–0.90, AC1 half-width is 0.017–0.058. Fidelity prevalence is a sensitivity assumption, not a lexical measurement. N80 held-out work is 126 h (31.5/person), versus 94.5 h at N60; total E1 including dev is 143.5 h. N80 is now the proposed size for the presence-primary design, not a claim of narrow fidelity κ.\n\nSecondary trace binary excludes partial/uncertain; sensitivity counts partial as lost. Both estimates and exclusions will be reported. Named human-label outputs `MQ_candidate_aggregation_failure_rate_human` and `SB_candidate_aggregation_failure_rate_human` address the 68% headline, using all traces as denominator and dev certainty units for full-frame rates. Human labels do not exist yet, so sensitivity conclusions cannot be asserted. Matcher v2 remains dev-only: 0/30 failed, 0/60 calls retried.\n\n'
 s+='## [R1]: released scorer decides primary\n\nPinned commit `14369dcb079c7cdf86ed2924145d5a28ff5c73b2`; downloaded source was verified byte-identical to the inspected cache.\n\n'
 s+='[parse.py, lines 169–170](https://github.com/ag2ai/whowhen_pro/blob/14369dcb079c7cdf86ed2924145d5a28ff5c73b2/whowhen_eval/parse.py#L169):\n```python\nif code in known:\n    return code, None\n```\n[score.py, line 152](https://github.com/ag2ai/whowhen_pro/blob/14369dcb079c7cdf86ed2924145d5a28ff5c73b2/whowhen_eval/score.py#L152):\n```python\n"mode": bool(pm) and pm in ok_modes,\n```\n'
 s+='**It accepts code-only answers. Released code-only scoring is primary; code+name strictness is sensitivity.** `Baseline-scoring-v4.md` records this source-based decision; neither prompt nor taxonomy changes. Released axes are independent: an invalid mode does not erase a correct agent or step. The adapter now invokes the pinned scorer directly.\n\n'
 rows=[]
 for r in scores['settings']:
  method,model,bench,exp=r['setting']
